          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "For uncomplicated urinary tract infections (UTIs) in non-pregnant adults, current guidance describes the following:\n\nAn **uncomplicated infection** is defined as acute cystitis in a non-pregnant adult who has a urinary tract that is structurally and functionally normal and a normal immune system. If any of these conditions are not met (e.g., pregnancy, male sex, catheters, structural abnormalities, kidney stones, weakened immune system, or suspected kidney involvement), the infection is considered complicated and requires a different management approach (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance, \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\").\n\nFor **diagnosis**, if a person has typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, it is appropriate to start treatment without a urine culture. In such cases, a urine dipstick test is not very helpful because a positive result confirms what is already likely, and a negative result does not reliably rule out an infection. Dipstick tests are more useful when symptoms are less clear (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance, \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
